WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Every pipeline stage makes assumptions about the stage before it.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.880
A filter may assume lines are present.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.780
A parser may assume a delimiter.

4
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.080
A summarizer may assume a column order.

5
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.340
A deployment step may assume the previous command selected the right artifact.

6
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.880
Those assumptions are where reliability and security meet.

7
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:29.920
Upstream output can be empty, malformed, truncated, localized, reordered, or unexpectedly large.

8
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:38.900
It may contain diagnostic text, duplicate records, missing fields, or values that look valid but do not mean what the next command expects.

9
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.720
A pipeline that never checks those conditions can make confident decisions from weak evidence.

10
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.800
Text boundaries are especially important.

11
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.460
Delimiters can appear inside values.

12
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.040
Columns can shift when a tool version changes.

13
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.460
Header rows can be added or removed.

14
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.320
Empty fields can be meaningful.

15
00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.340
Human-readable output may be designed for people, not for stable automation.

16
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:06.860
Secure pipeline design treats output from one command as input to another crossing a trust boundary.

17
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.600
The boundary does not need to imply an attacker.

18
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:17.060
It means the next stage should not blindly assume the previous stage produced exactly the expected shape of data.

19
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.620
When decisions matter, validate the output.

20
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.440
Check that required fields exist, counts are within expected ranges, and no-match conditions are handled deliberately.

21
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:33.300
Keep diagnostic messages away from machine-readable data, and stop safely when the pipeline cannot support the next action.

22
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.940
A good review question is: what must be true at this boundary for the next stage to be safe?

23
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.760
If the answer is only in someone's head, document it or encode it in checks.

24
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.460
That turns a brittle pipeline into a more trustworthy workflow.
